494
00:25:00,690 --> 00:25:04,190
let's be strategic over
what action we take when

495
00:25:04,190 --> 00:25:06,590
we're expanding in our tree.

496
00:25:06,590 --> 00:25:09,810
So when we decide to
sample, the next action

497
00:25:09,810 --> 00:25:12,380
doesn't have to be from
a default policy pi.

498
00:25:12,380 --> 00:25:14,180
Let's think carefully
about essentially

499
00:25:14,180 --> 00:25:17,600
where do we want to
fill in our search tree.

500
00:25:17,600 --> 00:25:19,670
And this is one of those
other really big ideas.

501
00:25:19,670 --> 00:25:20,630
Because this is
really where we're

502
00:25:20,630 --> 00:25:23,090
going to start to think about
ideas from reinforcement

503
00:25:23,090 --> 00:25:27,170
learning essentially to
optimize computation.

504
00:25:27,170 --> 00:25:29,150
Because right now
we're still assuming

505
00:25:29,150 --> 00:25:32,720
that we know the MDP, that we
know what the dynamics model is

506
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:34,590
and we know what
the reward model is.

507
00:25:34,590 --> 00:25:37,050
So in theory, if
computation was no issue,

508
00:25:37,050 --> 00:25:39,207
we could just do value backups.

509
00:25:39,207 --> 00:25:41,540
The challenge is this is going
to be completely enormous

510
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:43,280
and thus totally intractable.

511
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:47,330
So the idea here is to say,
well, maybe if we have access

512
00:25:47,330 --> 00:25:49,010
to those, we can
still think of trying

513
00:25:49,010 --> 00:25:51,230
to approximate sort of
like Bellman backups

514
00:25:51,230 --> 00:25:53,100
or approximate maxes.

515
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:54,920
But we don't
actually have to want

516
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:56,785
to enumerate all
the actions as much,

517
00:25:56,785 --> 00:25:58,160
and we want to
really focus where

518
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:00,560
we're using our computation.

519
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:02,360
And DeepMind has
been really a pioneer

520
00:26:02,360 --> 00:26:04,790
in thinking about using
reinforcement learning

521
00:26:04,790 --> 00:26:07,970
to prioritize computation,
to solve a lot of really

522
00:26:07,970 --> 00:26:08,850
important problems.

523
00:26:08,850 --> 00:26:11,550
And I'll try to come
back to that at the end.

524
00:26:11,550 --> 00:26:15,350
OK, so how does UCT work?

525
00:26:15,350 --> 00:26:17,720
The idea is, and this is why
I asked you guys about this

526
00:26:17,720 --> 00:26:19,580
and refresh your
understanding is,

527
00:26:19,580 --> 00:26:22,580
we are going to treat each
node, where each node that was

528
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:27,275
sort of like a state node inside
of our tree search as a bandit.

529
00:26:27,275 --> 00:26:29,900
And so it's like we have many,
many, many, many bandit problems

530
00:26:29,900 --> 00:26:32,450
inside of our search tree.

531
00:26:32,450 --> 00:26:35,540
And we're going to then maintain
an upper confidence bound

532
00:26:35,540 --> 00:26:39,110
of the reward of each
arm inside of a node.

533
00:26:39,110 --> 00:26:44,620
So the first node, you would
have is your root node.

534
00:26:47,310 --> 00:26:47,810
All right.

535
00:26:47,810 --> 00:26:51,880
And so it would have,
say, A1, A2, A3,

536
00:26:51,880 --> 00:26:55,370
and we would think of that as
a MAB, as a multi-armed bandit.

537
00:26:55,370 --> 00:26:58,720
And then when you get
further down in the tree,

538
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:02,680
so let's say we this
goes to next S prime.

539
00:27:02,680 --> 00:27:03,830
This would be another.

540
00:27:03,830 --> 00:27:04,760
A1, A2, A3.

541
00:27:08,240 --> 00:27:11,000
And this would be another
multi-armed bandit.

542
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,180
And you would have you'd have
to store in memory lots and lots

543
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:16,490
and lots of different
multi-armed bandits.

544
00:27:16,490 --> 00:27:19,620
So you're maintaining huge
numbers of multi-armed bandits.

545
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:22,530
And just like what we normally
do in upper confidence bound,

546
00:27:22,530 --> 00:27:25,700
we're going to maintain an upper
confidence bound over each arm.

547
00:27:25,700 --> 00:27:28,430
But we're going to be thinking
of that as essentially what

548
00:27:28,430 --> 00:27:31,640
would happen if I
take this action

549
00:27:31,640 --> 00:27:35,363
and then act optimally
till the end.

550
00:27:35,363 --> 00:27:37,030
Now, one big challenge
is, of course, we

551
00:27:37,030 --> 00:27:39,660
don't know what the reward
would be of acting optimally.

552
00:27:39,660 --> 00:27:43,280
So there's going to be a
lot of different policies

553
00:27:43,280 --> 00:27:44,760
that are moving at once.

554
00:27:44,760 --> 00:27:46,550
But let's see what
that might look like.

555
00:27:46,550 --> 00:27:49,120
So here's the idea.

556
00:27:49,120 --> 00:27:51,420
So let's say what
we're going to call.

557
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:52,920
We're going to say
we have a node I.

558
00:27:52,920 --> 00:27:56,030
So this could be our root node
or it could be any other node.

559
00:27:56,030 --> 00:28:00,780
The way we are going to I'm
just going to call this AI.

560
00:28:00,780 --> 00:28:04,200
We're going to try to maintain
an upper confidence bound

561
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:07,680
over what is the potential
expected discounted sum

562
00:28:07,680 --> 00:28:10,230
of rewards we'd get
starting in this node

563
00:28:10,230 --> 00:28:14,040
and taking this action
as the following.

564
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:16,530
Let's say that we've been
in that particular node

565
00:28:16,530 --> 00:28:18,600
and we have rolled
out from it using

566
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:20,100
some strategy that
we haven't really

567
00:28:20,100 --> 00:28:22,778
talked about yet, NIA times.

568
00:28:22,778 --> 00:28:25,320
So this is the number of times
we've been to this node before

569
00:28:25,320 --> 00:28:29,250
and we've happened to
expand the A action.

570
00:28:29,250 --> 00:28:31,650
What we do is we look
at all the returns

571
00:28:31,650 --> 00:28:33,640
we've gotten under those cases.

572
00:28:33,640 --> 00:28:34,930
So what's a return again?

573
00:28:34,930 --> 00:28:38,685
So a return would be,
you go back to here.

574
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:43,870
So let's say you've
done this, OK.

575
00:28:43,870 --> 00:28:46,390
What you would do, what your
return in this case would be is

576
00:28:46,390 --> 00:28:50,050
it would be a sum of
all of these rewards

577
00:28:50,050 --> 00:28:52,330
you've gotten along the way.

578
00:28:52,330 --> 00:28:55,360
So G we're going to use
to denote the return.

579
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:59,850
So this would be, this reward
from starting in state A,

580
00:28:59,850 --> 00:29:02,070
taking A1, and getting
the rewards out

581
00:29:02,070 --> 00:29:04,140
to the terminal state.

582
00:29:04,140 --> 00:29:07,180
And maybe next time you
go down this action,

583
00:29:07,180 --> 00:29:10,270
you actually get to here and
you get a different return.

584
00:29:10,270 --> 00:29:13,620
So those are just like your
Monte Carlo returns from before.

585
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:15,210
And it's just for
all the other times

586
00:29:15,210 --> 00:29:18,600
you went through that action.

587
00:29:18,600 --> 00:29:20,400
So that's part of it.

588
00:29:20,400 --> 00:29:23,090
So that's just an average and
it's kind of a weird average

589
00:29:23,090 --> 00:29:25,970
because it might be that
your which nodes you visited

590
00:29:25,970 --> 00:29:27,960
and which actions you
took have changed.

591
00:29:27,960 --> 00:29:30,930
So we're not committing it to
it to be a particular policy.

592
00:29:30,930 --> 00:29:33,170
It's just like we've
taken some action

593
00:29:33,170 --> 00:29:35,435
and we've followed-- we've
made a series of decisions

594
00:29:35,435 --> 00:29:36,810
until we got to
a terminal state.

595
00:29:36,810 --> 00:29:40,530
We added up the rewards and
we keep track of that here.

596
00:29:40,530 --> 00:29:43,280
So that's one thing and that's
sort of a we probably look at it

597
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:45,620
and think this is a very
loose approximation of what

598
00:29:45,620 --> 00:29:49,700
the optimal Q value is
for that state in action.

599
00:29:49,700 --> 00:29:54,035
The second term looks like upper
confidence bound, which is,

600
00:29:54,035 --> 00:29:57,480
you have some constant C/
you have some log term,

601
00:29:57,480 --> 00:30:00,500
which depends on the number
of times you visited this node

602
00:30:00,500 --> 00:30:04,430
divided by NIA, number of
times we've been in that node

603
00:30:04,430 --> 00:30:06,770
and taken that
particular action.

604
00:30:06,770 --> 00:30:08,960
So this just looks
like a bandit term.

605
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:12,910
It's an upper confidence bound
over the reward that we can get.

606
00:30:15,490 --> 00:30:18,400
And so what Upper
Confidence Tree does

607
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:21,490
is that the way it picks
the action, the next action

608
00:30:21,490 --> 00:30:24,820
to take from the current node is
it picks whichever one of these

609
00:30:24,820 --> 00:30:28,680
has this higher upper
confidence bound.

610
00:30:28,680 --> 00:30:30,690
Now, it should seem
slightly suspicious

611
00:30:30,690 --> 00:30:34,900
that this works because in
bandits, when we took an action,

612
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:39,030
we knew that this really was an
unbiased estimate of the reward

613
00:30:39,030 --> 00:30:41,740
of that action because we just
would see that one action.

614
00:30:41,740 --> 00:30:44,010
And then we knew from
Hoeffding that this really

615
00:30:44,010 --> 00:30:48,900
was an upper confidence bound
on the true value of that arm.

616
00:30:48,900 --> 00:30:50,880
But now we're in a
much more weird case,

617
00:30:50,880 --> 00:30:53,807
where we are thinking of this
for a sequence of actions

618
00:30:53,807 --> 00:30:54,640
we're going to take.

619
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:56,800
We're trying to do
expectations over states,

620
00:30:56,800 --> 00:31:00,570
and the actual actions we're
taking from this node onwards

621
00:31:00,570 --> 00:31:02,450
may not be optimal.

622
00:31:02,450 --> 00:31:05,130
One time we might go through--

623
00:31:05,130 --> 00:31:08,290
I'll draw it on the board.

624
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:11,350
Like one time we might
go through this zig zag.

625
00:31:11,350 --> 00:31:14,150
Another time we might
go through this zig zag.

626
00:31:14,150 --> 00:31:15,650
Another time we
might come back here

627
00:31:15,650 --> 00:31:18,040
and then we take a
different action.

628
00:31:18,040 --> 00:31:21,250
So it's not like we're not doing
one step of policy improvement

629
00:31:21,250 --> 00:31:22,258
here.

630
00:31:22,258 --> 00:31:24,550
We just have lots of different
things that we're trying

631
00:31:24,550 --> 00:31:26,920
and we're averaging over them.

632
00:31:26,920 --> 00:31:29,800
So you should be slightly
suspicious whether or not

633
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:32,770
this is going to be
doing a reasonable thing.

634
00:31:32,770 --> 00:31:34,520
But it's certainly
something you could do,

635
00:31:34,520 --> 00:31:36,670
something you could
imagine coding.

636
00:31:36,670 --> 00:31:39,860
And then we'll do
this many, many times.

637
00:31:39,860 --> 00:31:41,230
And then at the very end--

638
00:31:41,230 --> 00:31:44,690
so this will expand essentially
different parts of your tree.

639
00:31:44,690 --> 00:31:47,030
And when you're following
this, in particular,

640
00:31:47,030 --> 00:31:50,410
you're going to start to
expand parts of the tree which

641
00:31:50,410 --> 00:31:52,670
look promising more.

642
00:31:52,670 --> 00:31:55,210
So if this one happens
to have been getting--

643
00:31:55,210 --> 00:31:57,970
this one gets like
plus 100 and this gets

644
00:31:57,970 --> 00:32:01,370
plus 100 and this gets plus 90.

645
00:32:01,370 --> 00:32:04,550
Whereas let's say one other
time when you took this action,

646
00:32:04,550 --> 00:32:07,220
you went down here
and you got minus 10,

647
00:32:07,220 --> 00:32:09,570
well, then when the next time
you get to your root node,

648
00:32:09,570 --> 00:32:11,987
you're probably going to be
more likely to keep going down

649
00:32:11,987 --> 00:32:13,430
this path.

650
00:32:13,430 --> 00:32:18,440
So it's going to selectively
expand parts of your tree.

651
00:32:18,440 --> 00:32:21,440
It's not going to hit and you'll
have these unbalanced trees

652
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:24,997
where you'll often see parts of
things are getting filled in.

653
00:32:24,997 --> 00:32:27,330
And then maybe if something
else becomes more promising,

654
00:32:27,330 --> 00:32:29,038
you'll switch to
another part of the tree

655
00:32:29,038 --> 00:32:31,160
and fill in things there.

656
00:32:31,160 --> 00:32:33,200
So it's sort of this
unbalanced construction

657
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:34,800
of your forward search tree.

658
00:32:34,800 --> 00:32:36,710
And the way that
it's unbalanced is

659
00:32:36,710 --> 00:32:40,520
that using the Monte Carlo
aspect to approximate all

660
00:32:40,520 --> 00:32:41,577
the expectations.

661
00:32:41,577 --> 00:32:43,160
And you're using
this upper confidence

662
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:47,300
bound to of selectively
prioritize across your actions.

663
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:51,830
And that's what's going to help
with our enormous action space.

664
00:32:51,830 --> 00:32:53,390
Now, you still
might be concerned

665
00:32:53,390 --> 00:32:56,300
that when there's a really
enormous number of actions,

666
00:32:56,300 --> 00:32:58,290
like we're going to see
in Go in other cases,

667
00:32:58,290 --> 00:33:00,230
that this still isn't
going to be enough,

668
00:33:00,230 --> 00:33:07,430
because if the number of
actions you have is a million,

669
00:33:07,430 --> 00:33:09,980
these things generally--

670
00:33:09,980 --> 00:33:11,510
you'll have 0 for
this part right

671
00:33:11,510 --> 00:33:13,250
before you have
taken any actions

672
00:33:13,250 --> 00:33:16,070
and your counts will
all be the same.

673
00:33:16,070 --> 00:33:19,040
So, it should still be
concerning because what should

674
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:21,470
you do if you have these
1,000 different actions,

675
00:33:21,470 --> 00:33:25,340
and like you might not be able
to do anything essentially until

676
00:33:25,340 --> 00:33:27,950
you've visited everything once.

677
00:33:27,950 --> 00:33:30,050
Because before then,
as long as you've

678
00:33:30,050 --> 00:33:32,300
defined something that's a
reasonable upper confidence

679
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:34,680
bound, everything is
going to look awesome.

680
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:37,320
It's like action 99 will be
awesome/ action 100 will be

681
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:37,820
awesome.

682
00:33:37,820 --> 00:33:40,320
And so you'll have to sample
all of them at least once,

683
00:33:40,320 --> 00:33:43,040
and that generally will
be completely intractable.

684
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:46,308
So we'll see ways to
further reduce this.

685
00:33:46,308 --> 00:33:48,600
But what you can think of
this part is doing is saying,

686
00:33:48,600 --> 00:33:51,848
well, if you can at least
sample every action once,

687
00:33:51,848 --> 00:33:53,390
you can at least
mean that you're not

688
00:33:53,390 --> 00:33:55,678
going to have to focus on
unpromising actions later

689
00:33:55,678 --> 00:33:58,345
because you're going to quickly
use this upper confidence bound.

690
00:34:03,830 --> 00:34:06,150
So these sort of Monte
Carlo Tree Searches

691
00:34:06,150 --> 00:34:07,960
are starting to look
really promising.

692
00:34:07,960 --> 00:34:10,130
A lot of people have
used tree search

693
00:34:10,130 --> 00:34:11,883
based algorithms,
as some of you might

694
00:34:11,883 --> 00:34:14,300
have seen in other machine
learning algorithms or other AI

695
00:34:14,300 --> 00:34:17,030
classes probably in particular.

696
00:34:17,030 --> 00:34:20,449
But what this Monte Carlo
and UCT based approaches is,

697
00:34:20,449 --> 00:34:23,159
there's this highly
selective best first search,

698
00:34:23,159 --> 00:34:26,210
but with simulations as well.

699
00:34:26,210 --> 00:34:28,969
And they're using
sampling to break

700
00:34:28,969 --> 00:34:30,949
the curse of dimensionality.

701
00:34:30,949 --> 00:34:43,714
On UCT and UCT to help
with large action spaces.

702
00:34:49,270 --> 00:34:51,909
And the other really nice
benefit of these ones

703
00:34:51,909 --> 00:34:57,863
is they're parallelisable So
when you're sampling things,

704
00:34:57,863 --> 00:34:59,280
you could certainly
imagine trying

