00:36:39,220 --> 00:36:40,870
So what they ended
up doing is they

739
00:36:40,870 --> 00:36:42,880
are going to use
reinforcement learning

740
00:36:42,880 --> 00:36:44,350
to help solve this problem.

741
00:36:44,350 --> 00:36:46,850
We'll see whether or not the
technical difficulties resolve.

742
00:36:46,850 --> 00:36:48,790
And then what they
did is they started

743
00:36:48,790 --> 00:36:51,310
playing against grandmasters
and they tried to-- then

744
00:36:51,310 --> 00:36:53,560
they played against Lee
Sedol, who was one of the best

745
00:36:53,560 --> 00:36:55,120
people in the world at Go.

746
00:36:55,120 --> 00:36:57,970
And I think one of the really
interesting things about this

747
00:36:57,970 --> 00:36:59,980
is that it really
shows that A, it's

748
00:36:59,980 --> 00:37:02,830
now possible to use
AI to beat the best

749
00:37:02,830 --> 00:37:07,000
people in the world at Go, but
also the types of strategies

750
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:09,790
that it built were very
different than what people

751
00:37:09,790 --> 00:37:11,060
were doing before.

752
00:37:11,060 --> 00:37:14,470
And so I think this is a
pretty important aspect for AI

753
00:37:14,470 --> 00:37:18,310
because we've often thought of
AI as sort of automating things

754
00:37:18,310 --> 00:37:20,500
that people already
know how to do.

755
00:37:20,500 --> 00:37:23,278
And I think this illustrated
that they're really

756
00:37:23,278 --> 00:37:24,820
starting to be places
where computers

757
00:37:24,820 --> 00:37:29,030
go beyond even the best humans
and what we know how to do.

758
00:37:29,030 --> 00:37:31,760
And since then, there's
been a recent paper,

759
00:37:31,760 --> 00:37:33,910
I think maybe a year
or two ago by Been Kim

760
00:37:33,910 --> 00:37:36,910
trying to look whether or not
you can teach grandmasters

761
00:37:36,910 --> 00:37:41,920
using the strategies that
AlphaGo and its descendants

762
00:37:41,920 --> 00:37:42,868
invented.

763
00:37:42,868 --> 00:37:45,160
And so then there's this
really interesting opportunity

764
00:37:45,160 --> 00:37:47,740
and question to think
about, can we actually

765
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:49,780
learn from computers
in these new ways

766
00:37:49,780 --> 00:37:52,780
and try to exceed both human
level performance and computer

767
00:37:52,780 --> 00:37:54,070
level performance.

768
00:37:54,070 --> 00:37:56,300
So I will post this later.

769
00:37:56,300 --> 00:37:58,510
You guys can look at it.

770
00:37:58,510 --> 00:38:00,220
Let's go back to there.

771
00:38:00,220 --> 00:38:02,520
OK.

772
00:38:02,520 --> 00:38:03,280
All right.

773
00:38:03,280 --> 00:38:04,750
So how does Go work?

774
00:38:04,750 --> 00:38:06,780
Well, it's a really,
really old game.

775
00:38:06,780 --> 00:38:10,600
It's considered one of the
classic, hardest board games.

776
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:12,930
And it was considered a
grand challenge for AI

777
00:38:12,930 --> 00:38:15,270
for many, many decades.

778
00:38:15,270 --> 00:38:17,890
This sort of game tree
search that we saw before,

779
00:38:17,890 --> 00:38:19,650
something like a Ford search.

780
00:38:19,650 --> 00:38:21,840
Now, it couldn't be
expectimax in this case

781
00:38:21,840 --> 00:38:23,590
because it's a two player game.

782
00:38:23,590 --> 00:38:26,530
Go is what's considered
a zero sum game,

783
00:38:26,530 --> 00:38:28,750
meaning that someone
either wins and loses.

784
00:38:28,750 --> 00:38:31,480
And in this case, whenever
we think of a next state,

785
00:38:31,480 --> 00:38:33,280
rather than it being
an expectation,

786
00:38:33,280 --> 00:38:36,450
it's really a minimax
problem because each opponent

787
00:38:36,450 --> 00:38:38,850
is playing to win.

788
00:38:38,850 --> 00:38:43,320
So in this case, it's good to
think about what is actually

789
00:38:43,320 --> 00:38:46,240
uncertain in this case.

790
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:48,962
When we're playing Go, the
rules of the game are known.

791
00:38:48,962 --> 00:38:50,670
They actually have
another descendant now

792
00:38:50,670 --> 00:38:52,720
where you didn't have to
know the rules of the game.

793
00:38:52,720 --> 00:38:54,970
But certainly for the first
few, the rules of the game

794
00:38:54,970 --> 00:38:56,250
were known.

795
00:38:56,250 --> 00:38:58,380
So what's unknown
in Go, if we wanted

796
00:38:58,380 --> 00:39:05,410
to think about building a tree
or trying to learn in this case,

797
00:39:05,410 --> 00:39:07,100
if we know the rules--

798
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:08,670
Yeah.

799
00:39:08,670 --> 00:39:12,580
Well, you might expect your
adversary to play the best move.

800
00:39:12,580 --> 00:39:14,140
That might not always be true.

801
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:16,180
They might be seeking
different strategies,

802
00:39:16,180 --> 00:39:17,830
so you wouldn't know that.

803
00:39:17,830 --> 00:39:18,580
It's a good point.

804
00:39:18,580 --> 00:39:19,955
So it might be as
[MUTED] saying,

805
00:39:19,955 --> 00:39:23,580
that you might not know exactly
what the best strategy is,

806
00:39:23,580 --> 00:39:26,190
or you might not know
whether someone's going

807
00:39:26,190 --> 00:39:27,400
to play the best strategy.

808
00:39:27,400 --> 00:39:28,530
I think the other
thing that I think of

809
00:39:28,530 --> 00:39:30,822
is that we don't always know
what the best strategy is.

810
00:39:30,822 --> 00:39:33,430
It's just incredibly hard to
compute this in this case.

811
00:39:33,430 --> 00:39:36,460
And so in this case,
that's sort of next state,

812
00:39:36,460 --> 00:39:39,142
if that next state is
really from an adversary,

813
00:39:39,142 --> 00:39:41,100
it's not clear you've
got stochasticity in that

814
00:39:41,100 --> 00:39:43,980
because you don't know
what the optimal game is.

815
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:46,300
Now, of course, once
someone picks a move,

816
00:39:46,300 --> 00:39:47,740
everything is deterministic.

817
00:39:47,740 --> 00:39:50,080
So in some ways it's all
deterministic, it's all known.

818
00:39:50,080 --> 00:39:52,990
The key thing is that because
it's this adversarial game,

819
00:39:52,990 --> 00:39:55,680
it's not clear what the
optimal strategy is.

820
00:39:55,680 --> 00:39:59,060
So that's kind of one of
the really hard parts.

821
00:39:59,060 --> 00:39:59,560
All right.

822
00:39:59,560 --> 00:40:01,690
Just a couple basics
of the rules of Go.

823
00:40:01,690 --> 00:40:04,240
So normally it's played
on a 19 by 19 board.

824
00:40:04,240 --> 00:40:05,930
But when people first
started to-- well,

825
00:40:05,930 --> 00:40:09,760
kids and also when researchers
started tackling this game

826
00:40:09,760 --> 00:40:13,670
in earnest, starting like the
late 2000s or 2000, David Silva,

827
00:40:13,670 --> 00:40:16,580
who's one of the authors of
this and an amazing researcher,

828
00:40:16,580 --> 00:40:20,330
I think as part of his PhD
in the late 2008, 2009,

829
00:40:20,330 --> 00:40:23,890
he was doing things
on a 9 by 9 board.

830
00:40:23,890 --> 00:40:27,490
And just as a couple of basics,
there's two different players,

831
00:40:27,490 --> 00:40:30,530
either someone playing the black
stones or the white stones,

832
00:40:30,530 --> 00:40:34,120
and you're trying to surround
stones that are captured

833
00:40:34,120 --> 00:40:35,440
and then you win.

834
00:40:35,440 --> 00:40:42,350
It's as I said, it's a
zero-one game, zero-one game,

835
00:40:42,350 --> 00:40:44,297
which means a winner takes all.

836
00:40:44,297 --> 00:40:45,880
One of the interesting
things about Go

837
00:40:45,880 --> 00:40:48,520
is that in general, there's
no intermediate reward.

838
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:50,770
So you have to play
till the end of the game

839
00:40:50,770 --> 00:40:52,070
to see who's actually winning.

840
00:40:52,070 --> 00:40:53,410
And so there's just
a single reward

841
00:40:53,410 --> 00:40:55,600
at the end, which also makes
it very hard for credit

842
00:40:55,600 --> 00:40:59,185
assignment and to understand
what moves caused the resulting

843
00:40:59,185 --> 00:40:59,685
game.

844
00:41:02,300 --> 00:41:06,110
Yeah, so AlphaGo and
AlphaZero, AlphaGo

845
00:41:06,110 --> 00:41:08,040
was the first one that was used.

846
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:10,500
Then they developed
a number of variants.

847
00:41:10,500 --> 00:41:12,200
They then played
against Lee Sedol,

848
00:41:12,200 --> 00:41:14,390
and then there was AlphaZero.

849
00:41:14,390 --> 00:41:16,040
And what they
exhibit in this case

850
00:41:16,040 --> 00:41:18,810
is a number of different
really interesting features.

851
00:41:18,810 --> 00:41:22,640
So they have self-play,
strategic computation, highly

852
00:41:22,640 --> 00:41:24,210
selective best first search.

853
00:41:24,210 --> 00:41:26,010
They use the power of averaging.

854
00:41:26,010 --> 00:41:28,280
They leverage local computation.

855
00:41:28,280 --> 00:41:30,765
And then they learn
and update heuristics.

856
00:41:30,765 --> 00:41:33,140
For those of you that have
seen tree search based methods

857
00:41:33,140 --> 00:41:35,150
before, you've often
probably seen ideas

858
00:41:35,150 --> 00:41:38,210
around heuristics, which are
other ways to think about

859
00:41:38,210 --> 00:41:39,930
how do you expand the tree.

860
00:41:39,930 --> 00:41:42,522
One of the interesting
ideas in these papers

861
00:41:42,522 --> 00:41:44,480
is that they're going to
learn those heuristics

862
00:41:44,480 --> 00:41:46,220
and update them over time.

863
00:41:46,220 --> 00:41:48,210
It's another important aspect.

864
00:41:48,210 --> 00:41:50,600
So let's see how it works.

865
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:53,930
So how does self-play work?

866
00:41:53,930 --> 00:41:56,090
So the key idea in this
case is that we're going

867
00:41:56,090 --> 00:41:58,572
to have the agent play itself.

868
00:41:58,572 --> 00:42:00,030
So there's going
to-- you can think

869
00:42:00,030 --> 00:42:04,140
of it as there being two copies
of this same agent right now.

870
00:42:04,140 --> 00:42:06,660
And what will happen when
they're playing a game

871
00:42:06,660 --> 00:42:08,850
is they compute the best
move of the current state

872
00:42:08,850 --> 00:42:10,600
and then the opponent
does the same.

873
00:42:10,600 --> 00:42:13,320
And they have access
essentially to the same policy

874
00:42:13,320 --> 00:42:16,050
or the same sort of algorithm,
but they're both just

875
00:42:16,050 --> 00:42:19,020
using it in an adversarial way.

876
00:42:19,020 --> 00:42:21,330
And so that means the only
bottleneck in this case

877
00:42:21,330 --> 00:42:22,150
is computation.

878
00:42:22,150 --> 00:42:24,360
We have no humans involved.

879
00:42:24,360 --> 00:42:28,430
And self-play also provides
a well-matched player.

880
00:42:28,430 --> 00:42:30,440
So take a second
and think about,

881
00:42:30,440 --> 00:42:35,240
what are the benefits that
will happen with self-play

882
00:42:35,240 --> 00:42:37,840
and what's going to be
like the reward density.

883
00:42:37,840 --> 00:42:40,340
Are there going to be lots of
rewards when you do self-play?

884
00:42:40,340 --> 00:42:41,970
Are there going to be
very little rewards?

885
00:42:41,970 --> 00:42:43,400
Let's just take a second
and I'll check and see

886
00:42:43,400 --> 00:42:44,942
whether I can make
the networks work.

887
00:42:53,940 --> 00:42:56,190
Maybe talk to a neighbor and
see if you guys both have

888
00:42:56,190 --> 00:42:58,230
the same idea of whether
self-play will be helpful

889
00:42:58,230 --> 00:42:58,730
or not.

890
00:43:12,750 --> 00:43:13,250
All right.

891
00:43:13,250 --> 00:43:15,590
What does this do
to policy training?

892
00:43:15,590 --> 00:43:17,150
What happens when
you do self-play?

893
00:43:21,170 --> 00:43:22,890
Do you have higher
reward density.

894
00:43:22,890 --> 00:43:24,343
Do you have low reward density?

895
00:43:24,343 --> 00:43:24,885
What happens?

896
00:43:29,953 --> 00:43:31,370
Here, raise your
hand if you think

897
00:43:31,370 --> 00:43:34,290
you have high reward density.

898
00:43:34,290 --> 00:43:36,770
If you think you have
low reward density.

899
00:43:36,770 --> 00:43:38,497
So, all right.

900
00:43:38,497 --> 00:43:40,580
Was somebody who think we
have high reward density

901
00:43:40,580 --> 00:43:42,118
you want to explain why.

902
00:43:42,118 --> 00:43:42,660
That's right.

903
00:43:42,660 --> 00:43:44,118
We do a pretty high
reward density.

904
00:43:44,118 --> 00:43:46,940
Why do we get that
when we do self-play?

905
00:43:46,940 --> 00:43:49,577
What happens?

906
00:43:49,577 --> 00:43:51,410
Or I think it's easy,
maybe easiest to think

907
00:43:51,410 --> 00:43:54,770
of like if you play against
someone that's much, much

908
00:43:54,770 --> 00:43:56,965
better than you, what happens?

909
00:44:00,260 --> 00:44:01,655
Just kind of lose-lose.

910
00:44:01,655 --> 00:44:02,835
Lose all the time, right?

911
00:44:02,835 --> 00:44:04,710
I mean, everyone's
probably done this before.

912
00:44:04,710 --> 00:44:06,320
You play against like a friend
of yours that maybe much

913
00:44:06,320 --> 00:44:08,520
better at a board game than
you or something like that.

914
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:10,370
Or you're a better friend of
yours is much better than you

915
00:44:10,370 --> 00:44:12,150
at tennis or something, and
you go and play with them.

916
00:44:12,150 --> 00:44:14,108
And it's not normally
that fun because you just

917
00:44:14,108 --> 00:44:15,830
lose all the time.

918
00:44:15,830 --> 00:44:17,595
And when you lose
all the time, you

919
00:44:17,595 --> 00:44:19,220
may not get very much
signal about what

920
00:44:19,220 --> 00:44:20,762
things are even
doing better or worse

921
00:44:20,762 --> 00:44:22,427
at because you always lose.

922
00:44:22,427 --> 00:44:24,260
And so that would be a
case where the reward

923
00:44:24,260 --> 00:44:27,810
density is very low because the
players are really mismatched.

924
00:44:27,810 --> 00:44:31,010
And it means that most of the
time the agent is not winning.

925
00:44:31,010 --> 00:44:33,410
Now the same thing is true
if the agent is much better

926
00:44:33,410 --> 00:44:34,800
than the other agent.

927
00:44:34,800 --> 00:44:37,773
But self-play means you're
sort of matched cheaters,

928
00:44:37,773 --> 00:44:40,190
like matched at the same level
as someone who plays tennis

929
00:44:40,190 --> 00:44:42,140
the same level as
you, or you're matched

930
00:44:42,140 --> 00:44:44,270
with someone who has the
same Helo score as you

931
00:44:44,270 --> 00:44:47,870
and in chess or Go,
which is sort of a way

932
00:44:47,870 --> 00:44:49,522
to quantify the player's skill.

933
00:44:49,522 --> 00:44:51,980
And the nice thing about that
is that you would expect that

934
00:44:51,980 --> 00:44:54,530
roughly, if you play someone
that's exactly the same level--

935
00:44:54,530 --> 00:44:56,113
now here you're an
Rl agent, so you're

936
00:44:56,113 --> 00:44:59,140
going to play someone that's
actually just on the other side.

937
00:44:59,140 --> 00:45:01,120
So they're exactly
the same level as you,

938
00:45:01,120 --> 00:45:04,390
and that means you'd expect
you'd win about half the time.

939
00:45:04,390 --> 00:45:05,730
I think, on average.

940
00:45:05,730 --> 00:45:10,650
So that's really good density
for something that is a zero-one

941
00:45:10,650 --> 00:45:14,430
game because you're not just
like every 3,000 games getting

942
00:45:14,430 --> 00:45:17,005
a zero or a one here.

943
00:45:17,005 --> 00:45:18,880
About half the time
you'd expect to get a one

944
00:45:18,880 --> 00:45:21,340
and half the time you'd
expect to get a zero.

945
00:45:21,340 --> 00:45:22,980
And the reason that
might be beneficial

946
00:45:22,980 --> 00:45:24,522
is hopefully that's
going to give you

947
00:45:24,522 --> 00:45:26,040
a lot more signal
of how you should

948
00:45:26,040 --> 00:45:30,510
change your policy in order to
figure out how to get better.

949
00:45:30,510 --> 00:45:32,780
So I think that self-play
is a really interesting one

950
00:45:32,780 --> 00:45:34,530
because you could think
of it in some ways

951
00:45:34,530 --> 00:45:38,160
as kind of providing an
automatic curriculum.

952
00:45:38,160 --> 00:45:39,180
Go to the next one.

953
00:45:39,180 --> 00:45:41,447
The rewards are going
to be pretty dense.

954
00:45:41,447 --> 00:45:44,030
And for those of you that have
seen curriculum learning before

955
00:45:44,030 --> 00:45:47,330
in other machine learning stuff,
just like in classes where

956
00:45:47,330 --> 00:45:49,490
you often build up
with math over time

957
00:45:49,490 --> 00:45:51,000
and you don't start
with calculus.

958
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:53,550
You start with addition
or what a number is,

959
00:45:53,550 --> 00:45:54,930
and then you slowly build up.

960
00:45:54,930 --> 00:45:57,630
So you're always trying to be
on roughly the right level.

961
00:45:57,630 --> 00:46:01,093
Similarly here, the agent
should do that automatically

962
00:46:01,093 --> 00:46:02,510
because they're
going to start off

963
00:46:02,510 --> 00:46:04,460
and they're going to
both be terrible at Go,

964
00:46:04,460 --> 00:46:06,960
but they're still going to get
pretty high density of reward

965
00:46:06,960 --> 00:46:08,750
because they're
both terrible at Go.

966
00:46:08,750 --> 00:46:11,510
And then over time, the
agents are going to get better

967
00:46:11,510 --> 00:46:13,220
and then now they're
automatically

968
00:46:13,220 --> 00:46:17,330
always playing an agent that's
roughly the same level as them.

969
00:46:17,330 --> 00:46:19,220
Now, we'll have to see
why the algorithm will

970
00:46:19,220 --> 00:46:20,190
help them get better.

971
00:46:20,190 --> 00:46:24,180
But intuitively, as we saw, even
with the Monte Carlo simulation,

972
00:46:24,180 --> 00:46:26,090
not even tree
search, there, it was

973
00:46:26,090 --> 00:46:28,380
doing like one step
of policy improvement.

974
00:46:28,380 --> 00:46:30,710
So you can imagine that
even if each round were just

975
00:46:30,710 --> 00:46:33,990
doing of one step of policy
improvement, over time,

976
00:46:33,990 --> 00:46:36,650
we would hope that we're going
to get better and better.

977
00:46:36,650 --> 00:46:38,960
So this idea of
self-play I think

978
00:46:38,960 --> 00:46:40,550
is a really interesting one.

979
00:46:40,550 --> 00:46:44,588
It works really well in games
and it's been exploited a lot.

980
00:46:44,588 --> 00:46:47,130
I've often thought like it would
be really interesting to see

981
00:46:47,130 --> 00:46:48,780
are there other
places you can set up

982
00:46:48,780 --> 00:46:50,640
to essentially be like a game.

